WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.443
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level two.

2
00:00:05.743 --> 00:00:09.246
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.546 --> 00:00:18.177
Unit nineteen: Global Trade Disputes. Today you will use verbs with dependent prepositions: impose on, retaliate against, ban on.

4
00:00:18.477 --> 00:00:21.234
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.468
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.317 --> 00:00:27.399
Trade war.

7
00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:32.624
Trade war means a conflict in which countries raise tariffs against each other.

8
00:00:33.217 --> 00:00:35.669
The trade war disrupted many industries.

9
00:00:36.283 --> 00:00:39.052
Your turn. Say: Trade war.

10
00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:42.750
Trade war.

11
00:00:43.517 --> 00:00:45.156
Retaliatory tariff.

12
00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:51.482
Retaliatory tariff means a tax placed on imports as a response to another country's tariff.

13
00:00:52.083 --> 00:00:54.930
A retaliatory tariff hit farm exports.

14
00:00:55.517 --> 00:00:58.796
Your turn. Say: Retaliatory tariff.

15
00:01:01.633 --> 00:01:03.308
Retaliatory tariff.

16
00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.212
World Trade Organization.

17
00:01:07.217 --> 00:01:11.898
World Trade Organization means the international body that supervises trade rules.

18
00:01:12.483 --> 00:01:15.249
The World Trade Organization heard the complaint.

19
00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:19.570
Your turn. Say: World Trade Organization.

20
00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:24.305
World Trade Organization.

21
00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.492
Import restriction.

22
00:01:27.483 --> 00:01:32.304
Import restriction means a limit on the amount or type of goods brought into a country.

23
00:01:32.917 --> 00:01:35.845
An import restriction reduced steel supplies.

24
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:39.323
Your turn. Say: Import restriction.

25
00:01:42.167 --> 00:01:43.667
Import restriction.

26
00:01:44.417 --> 00:01:45.847
Trade negotiation.

27
00:01:46.850 --> 00:01:50.370
Trade negotiation means formal talks to agree trade rules.

28
00:01:50.983 --> 00:01:53.505
The trade negotiation lasted two years.

29
00:01:54.117 --> 00:01:57.512
Your turn. Say: Trade negotiation.

30
00:02:00.367 --> 00:02:01.879
Trade negotiation.

31
00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:03.850
Export ban.

32
00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:08.777
Export ban means an official rule forbidding goods to be sent abroad.

33
00:02:09.383 --> 00:02:12.079
An export ban stopped grain shipments.

34
00:02:12.683 --> 00:02:15.684
Your turn. Say: Export ban.

35
00:02:18.533 --> 00:02:19.883
Export ban.

36
00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:22.198
Economic sanction.

37
00:02:23.183 --> 00:02:27.888
Economic sanction means a penalty on a country to force it to change its behaviour.

38
00:02:28.483 --> 00:02:31.307
Economic sanctions froze the bank's assets.

39
00:02:31.917 --> 00:02:35.196
Your turn. Say: Economic sanction.

40
00:02:38.033 --> 00:02:39.487
Economic sanction.

41
00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:41.541
Bilateral agreement.

42
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:45.560
Bilateral agreement means a deal between two countries.

43
00:02:46.150 --> 00:02:48.672
A bilateral agreement ended the dispute.

44
00:02:49.283 --> 00:02:52.133
Your turn. Say: Bilateral agreement.

45
00:02:54.967 --> 00:02:56.606
Bilateral agreement.

46
00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:00.923
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:01.783 --> 00:03:09.695
Visal, the report says 'The government imposed a tariff on steel, and its partner retaliated against the move.' Which words need prepositions?

48
00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:20.959
Impose ... on, retaliate against, a ban on, a dispute over, negotiate with. These dependent prepositions must be learned together.

49
00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.957
Why do they matter in trade news?

50
00:03:25.050 --> 00:03:36.621
Precision. 'A ban on exports' is correct; 'a ban of exports' is not. Writers also say sanctions against a country and an agreement between two states.

51
00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:39.356
How does a trade war end?

52
00:03:40.450 --> 00:03:49.975
Usually through trade negotiation. The World Trade Organization may rule on the dispute, or a bilateral agreement removes the tariffs.

53
00:03:51.133 --> 00:03:52.773
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:53.617 --> 00:03:57.079
Grammar focus: Dependent prepositions in trade English.

55
00:03:57.379 --> 00:03:58.601
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:59.317 --> 00:04:01.943
The state imposed a tariff on imported steel.

57
00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:05.221
Its partner retaliated against the move.

58
00:04:06.117 --> 00:04:08.894
The export ban on grain caused shortages.

59
00:04:09.833 --> 00:04:19.196
Learn the preposition with the word: impose ON, retaliate AGAINST, a ban ON, a dispute OVER, sanctions AGAINST. Do not translate prepositions word for word.

60
00:04:19.950 --> 00:04:24.007
Be careful! Don't say: The state imposed a tariff of steel.

61
00:04:25.967 --> 00:04:28.616
Say: The state imposed a tariff on steel.

62
00:04:28.916 --> 00:04:31.055
Impose takes the preposition on.

63
00:04:31.867 --> 00:04:34.647
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:37.410
When Trade Turns Into a Fight.

65
00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:42.240
A trade war begins when one country imposes a tariff on another country's goods.

66
00:04:43.233 --> 00:04:49.296
The partner often retaliates against the move with a retaliatory tariff, and an import restriction may follow.

67
00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:54.575
An export ban on grain or fuel can quickly raise prices worldwide.

68
00:04:55.567 --> 00:05:01.444
The World Trade Organization may rule on the dispute, but a bilateral agreement is usually the real solution.

69
00:05:02.433 --> 00:05:05.269
Patient trade negotiation is cheaper than conflict.

70
00:05:06.367 --> 00:05:10.403
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:13.993
The state imposed a tariff blank imported steel.

72
00:05:14.293 --> 00:05:16.773
A, on. B, at. C, of.

73
00:05:20.333 --> 00:05:21.787
The answer is A: on.

74
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.309
Its partner retaliated blank the move.

75
00:05:25.609 --> 00:05:28.578
A, against. B, to. C, on.

76
00:05:32.133 --> 00:05:34.272
The answer is A: against.

77
00:05:35.417 --> 00:05:38.032
A deal between two countries is a blank agreement.

78
00:05:38.332 --> 00:05:42.658
A, bilateral. B, global. C, restrictive.

79
00:05:46.200 --> 00:05:48.443
The answer is A: bilateral.

80
00:05:49.583 --> 00:05:51.188
Choose the correct phrase.

81
00:05:51.488 --> 00:05:57.162
A, a ban of exports. B, a ban on exports. C, a ban to exports.

82
00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:03.524
The answer is B: a ban on exports.

83
00:06:04.683 --> 00:06:08.575
According to the reading, the real solution is usually a bilateral…

84
00:06:08.875 --> 00:06:12.285
A, agreement. B, war. C, sanction.

85
00:06:15.833 --> 00:06:17.949
The answer is A: agreement.

86
00:06:19.133 --> 00:06:22.076
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:22.917 --> 00:06:27.505
How might a trade war between two large countries affect prices in a third country?

88
00:06:36.317 --> 00:06:37.875
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:38.175 --> 00:07:02.427
A trade war can raise prices in a third country in several ways. Companies in the two large countries may sell less to each other, so they look for new buyers and compete for the same goods. Supply chains can also be disrupted, which increases costs. On the other hand, a third country may gain cheaper goods if the two rivals dump their surplus there.

90
00:07:02.727 --> 00:07:04.413
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:05.317 --> 00:07:10.117
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and dependent prepositions in trade english.

92
00:07:10.417 --> 00:07:16.262
Today's value is global economic literacy. Understand how money and trade connect the world.

93
00:07:16.562 --> 00:07:18.901
See you in Unit twenty. Goodbye!

94
00:07:19.201 --> 00:07:22.677
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

